# 64-bit integer arithmetic WRAPS on overflow, on every backend

The runtime companion to [`int-width`](int-width.ssc). That case pins the
*width* of `Int`/`Long` (64 bits, [`specs/numeric-widths.md`](../../specs/numeric-widths.md) §2);
this one pins what happens at the **edge** of that width: `+ - * / %` wrap
modulo 2⁶⁴, exactly like the interpreter and the JVM. A backend that keeps
growing the number instead is non-conforming.

Every value here is COMPUTED in a loop, never folded. That is the point: the
front end already wraps compile-time constants, so a constant-folded case is
green on a lane whose *runtime* arithmetic is unbounded — it proves nothing
about the code an actual program runs.

**Why this case exists.** Measured 2026-07-31: the JS runtime's `_arith`
masked its bitwise ops with `BigInt.asIntN(64, …)` but returned raw, unbounded
`BigInt` for arithmetic. On the LCG below the JS lane printed
`8196388553494059765830957764855533743` for the second step where every other
lane printed `-6658320863940243281` — and because a BigInt gains ~61 bits per
iteration and never stops growing, the
`tuple-monoid` benchmark grew `s` to 6,131,220 bits and one 100k-iteration pass
cost **43.5 s instead of 5 ms**. Wrong answers and an 8700× slowdown from one
missing mask (`BUGS.md` → `js-long-arith-no-64bit-wrap`).

```scalascript
// An LCG — the canonical multiply-overflow shape. No closed form, so no lane
// can fold it; the multiply overflows on the very first step.
var s = 1L
var i = 0
while i < 5 do
  s = s * 2862933555777941757L + 3037000493L
  println(s)
  i = i + 1

// The low bits survive wrapping, so `% 4` is the cheap witness that the
// *whole* value wrapped rather than just printing something plausible.
println(s % 4L)

// The edges of the range, reached by doubling rather than by a literal.
var min = 1L
var k = 0
while k < 63 do
  min = min * 2L
  k = k + 1
println(min)              // 2^63 wraps to Long.MinValue
println(min - 1L)         // ... and one below it is Long.MaxValue
println(min / (0L - 1L))  // MinValue / -1 OVERFLOWS back to MinValue (no throw)
println(min % (0L - 1L))  // ... with a zero remainder

// Repeated multiply well past the top of the range.
var big = 1L
var j = 0
while j < 40 do
  big = big * 3L
  j = j + 1
println(big)

// ── the TWIN, and the reason this half is in the same file ────────────────────
// `BigInt` is arbitrary-precision and must NOT wrap. On the JS lane it shares a
// representation with `Long` (both are a JS `bigint`), so the two halves are one
// decision: the first attempt at the fix above masked in the shared `_arith` and
// silently truncated this value from 1e36 to -5527149226598858752. Keeping both
// checks in ONE case means neither can be "fixed" by breaking the other.
val b = BigInt(1000000000)
var p = b
var m = 0
while m < 3 do
  p = p * b
  m = m + 1
println(p)
```
